From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 02:13:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 15/49] mobile: Korean typed on the iPhone keyboard arrives
 once, without a stray 1

The iOS Korean keyboard rewrites the hidden field in several steps for one key
press: it removes the syllable and one filler character, puts the filler back,
then inserts the new syllable (or deletes and retypes the whole word). The
handler diffed every step, so it deleted a character the user had typed before
and typed the restored filler '1' (rustdesk issue #8786), and replayed whole
words per key.

The field is now diffed once it has settled: a controller listener (it also
sees commits that change only the composing range) restarts a 30 ms timer on
every change, flushed at the latest 100 ms after the first one. The new pure
function iosSoftKeyboardEdit() diffs by common prefix, waits on the
filler-minus-one step should it arrive alone, ignores one filler character put
back too many before a syllable on an otherwise empty field (the pattern and
the condition of rustdesk PR #15201), never splits a surrogate pair, counts
Backspaces in code points, and keeps sending Hangul while it is composed
(pinyin and kana are still held until converted). A held Backspace that
deletes a whole word takes the filler with it: when the word was text typed
in the field, the remote side deletes only that text; the filler alone
deletes at most 8 remote characters per step, and no edit sends more than 32.
Return inside an insertion is sent as a key. The caret is kept at the end of
the hidden field (two keys pressed at once or the space-bar trackpad moved it,
and typing there replayed filler), and the field is refilled when the filler
runs low, so a held Backspace keeps working. Smart quotes and dashes are off,
so ' " and -- arrive as typed.

The text a settling field holds back is sent before any key or click
(InputModel.flushSoftKeyboardText), so a key-bar key or a tap right after
typing does not overtake it.

iOS only: Android keeps its onChanged path.

(Research patch ios-keyboard A, hardened per its verification and the plan
review.)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
